From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 21 Jun 2026 12:09:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/16] media: cedrus: vc1: Skip the picture header 24 bits at
 a time

Positioning the bit reader at the start of the macroblock data was done
one bit per GET_BITS trigger, i.e. data_bit_offset register-write +
poll pairs per frame (often hundreds), which the code itself flagged as
a TODO. The engine's bit reader accepts up to 24-bit GET_BITS reads
(the vendor reads 24-bit start codes the same way) and strips
emulation-prevention bytes for any read width, so flush up to 24 bits
per trigger, with a smaller final chunk for the remainder. This cuts
the number of register writes by ~24x with no change in decoded output
(the full sample set remains bit-exact against the software decoder).
Wider reads (e.g. 32-bit) overrun the bit FIFO and desync the VLD, so
24 is the maximum.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tj82JZ5jPRRbNkByAQPcy7
Developed-by: Piotr Oniszczuk <piotr.oniszczuk@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: Piotr Oniszczuk <piotr.oniszczuk@gmail.com>

[Ported to the VC-1 stateless uAPI revision 3 (gus bourg): the bits to
skip are the start code (Advanced profile) plus data_bit_offset of the
picture layer control; the chunk counter is called "bits" because "skip"
already holds the total.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_vc1.c | 17 ++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_vc1.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_vc1.c
index 623c3ae64..ece333c4b 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_vc1.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_vc1.c
@@ -165,7 +165,7 @@ static int cedrus_vc1_setup(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx, struct cedrus_run *run)
 	struct cedrus_dev *dev = ctx->dev;
 	struct vb2_buffer *backward_vb2;
 	struct vb2_buffer *forward_vb2;
-	unsigned int raw_coding, skip;
+	unsigned int raw_coding, skip, bits;
 	dma_addr_t src_buf_addr;
 	u32 reg, condover, pq;
 	struct vb2_queue *vq;
@@ -318,11 +318,18 @@ static int cedrus_vc1_setup(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx, struct cedrus_run *run)
 	if (advanced)
 		skip += VC1_START_CODE_BITS;
 
-	/* TODO: minimize time impact of this block */
-	for (reg = 0; reg < skip; reg++) {
+	/*
+	 * Flush up to 24 bits per GET_BITS trigger: the engine's bit reader
+	 * handles <= 24-bit reads (the vendor reads 24-bit start codes the same
+	 * way) and strips emulation-prevention bytes, so this matches a 1-bit
+	 * loop but is far fewer register writes. Wider (e.g. 32-bit) reads
+	 * overrun the bit FIFO and desync the VLD.
+	 */
+	for (reg = 0; reg < skip; reg += bits) {
+		bits = min_t(u32, skip - reg, 24);
 		cedrus_write(dev, VE_DEC_VC1_TRIGGER_TYPE,
-				VE_DEC_VC1_TRIGGER_TYPE_GET_BITS |
-				VE_DEC_VC1_TRIGGER_TYPE_N_BITS(1));
+			     VE_DEC_VC1_TRIGGER_TYPE_GET_BITS |
+			     VE_DEC_VC1_TRIGGER_TYPE_N_BITS(bits));
 
 		cedrus_wait_for(dev, VE_DEC_VC1_STATUS, VE_DEC_VC1_STATUS_BITS_BUSY);
 	}
